# core.autocrlf is `true` in this checkout, so by default git rewrites LF to CRLF
# on checkout and back on commit. That is harmless for source it can round-trip,
# and destructive for a file whose bytes are the point.
#
# supabase/rollback/ holds byte-exact captures of production function
# definitions, recorded with an md5 that must still match after a clone. Some of
# those bodies contain literal CR characters of their own (123 of them in the
# 2026-09-01 capture); left to autocrlf, git would normalise those away on commit
# and re-expand every LF on checkout, and the restored functions would not be the
# functions that were captured. Marked as binary so nothing touches them.
supabase/rollback/prod_secdef_functions_*.sql -text -diff

# Note for anyone comparing a migration file against the recorded SQL in
# supabase_migrations: the working tree carries CRLF here and the database
# carries LF, so a digest comparison must normalise line endings first. See
# scripts/check-migrations.mjs and docs/TENANT_GUARD_REPORT.md §9.10.
